From ua032573fbe907edd at groups.google.com  Wed May 25 09:08:21 2011
From: ua032573fbe907edd at groups.google.com (Amukelani Shiringani)
Date: Wed, 25 May 2011 09:08:21 +0000
Subject: Re: [Rqtl-disc] mapping using F2 intercross of natural populations
Message-ID: <6CGk3Y3yMRc.O650tIZTLdAJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <6CGk3Y3yMRc.eeoyOaW6nl4J@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
References: <6CGk3Y3yMRc.rbvWoNFz4BwJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com> <6CGk3Y3yMRc.eeoyOaW6nl4J@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/6CGk3Y3yMRc/m/O650tIZTLdAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi there,

I have been following this topic since I have the same population, of f2 small families from selfed F1 (f1 from the same parents). I did what Haili wrote, group all AAxBB to create a rough map because i thought all individual at the same loci went the same number of recombination. Karl just replied that he wouldn't do that. I know this might not be a statistical or RQTL question, but can someone help me understand why?

Thank you
Amu

On Tue, May 24, 2011 at 5:32 PM, Karl Broman <kbr...@gmail.com> wrote:

>
> On May 24, 2011, at 11:14 AM, Haili Zhang wrote:
>
> > So, if I use multiple F2 intercrosses from different F1 pairs (single F0 pair), I can only use those markers that are AA and BB in F0, right?
>
> I wouldn't want to do that.
>
> > If I use single F2 familty for a phase-known-4-way mapping, how should I code the genotype in the read.cross function? Since I'm using SNP markers, the F1 cross pair is actually (AxB) x (AxB), rather than (AxB) x (CxD). Could you give me an example read.cross script that describe this kind of genotype?
>
> You need to infer phase, and there's a complicated encoding described in the help file for read.cross (in the section "Value" under "geno".
>
> Call the SNP alleles 1 and 2.  For a marker that is (1x2)x(1x2), the offspring genotypes are
>
>  11 = AC -> 1
>  22 = BD -> 4
>  12 = AD or BC -> 10
>
> If you create a CSV file with this encoding, you can read it in using read.cross, and it should be interpreted as a phase-known 4-way cross (cross type "4way").
>
> You can potentially do a combined analysis of multiple F2 families with R/qtl, but it's not documented and beyond what I want to support at this stage.  That is, it can be done, but you'll have to figure it out on your own, as my time is limited.
>
> karl

